WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 292: Edward the Confessor: Westminster Abbey, the Norman Vow, and the Disputed Crown of 1066

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:20.960
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series examines the pious and saintly king whose religious devotion gave England Westminster Abbey, but whose childless demise plunged the realm into the Norman conquest of 1066.

4
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:27.600
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of Edward the Confessor.

5
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:36.160
[George] Born around 1003 at Islip in Oxfordshire, Edward was the son of King Ethelred the Unready and his Norman Queen, Emma.

6
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:49.360
[George] When the Danish conqueror Swain Forkbeard invaded England in 1013, ten-year-old Prince Edward was whisked across the stormy English Channel to the safety of his mother's ancestral homeland, the Duchy of Normandy.

7
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.760
[George] For 25 long years, while C.

8
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:59.680
[George] Nut and his sons ruled England, Edward lived as an obscure, impoverished royal refugee in Rouen and Norman monasteries.

9
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:18.960
[George] Surrounded by Norman knights, Latin scholars and Benedictine monks, Edward absorbed the French language, Norman culture, and a deep, ascetic Catholic piety, developing a temperament far better suited to the cloistered quiet of a monastic abbey than the brutal, cutthroat politics of Anglo-Saxon kingship.

10
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:32.080
[George] Edward's 25-year exile in Normandy was a formative exile of isolation and quiet scholarly contemplation, living at the ducal court in Rouen and the ancient Benedictine abbeys of Fay Camp and Jumigas.

11
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:39.200
[George] Edward watched successive generations of Norman dukes consolidate their iron feudal grip over northwestern France.

12
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:53.760
[George] Accompanied by his mother Emma and younger brother Alfred, Edward developed a deep, lifelong affection for Norman culture, the French language, and the reformed Benedictine monastic revival championed by Abbot William of Valpiano.

13
00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:13.880
[George] When his younger brother Prince Alfred sailed to England in 1036 to claim the crown and was captured, blinded, and murdered at Ely, the trauma left an indelible scar on Edward's soul, instilling in him a deep, visceral suspicion of the powerful Anglo-Saxon nobility who had facilitated his brother's brutal slaughter.

14
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:27.800
[George] In 1042, following the sudden death of Senut's last son Harthnut, the English people, weary of Danish rule, erupted in joyous celebration and recalled the exiled Saxon prince to his father's throne.

15
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:43.080
[George] Crowned at Winchester Cathedral on Easter Day 1043, forty-year-old Edward found his royal authority severely constrained by the colossal power of Godwin, the mighty Earl of Wessex, who had accumulated vast wealth under Danish rule.

16
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:50.760
[George] To secure his throne, Edward was compelled to marry Godwin's beautiful, intelligent daughter Edith in 1045.

17
00:02:50.760 --> 00:03:02.440
[George] However, Edward harbored a deep, unyielding resentment toward Godwin, suspecting the Earl of complicity in the brutal blinding and murder of his younger brother Prince Alfred in 1036.

18
00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:17.160
[George] To counterbalance Godwin's overbearing Saxon clan, Edward surrounded himself with trusted Norman advisers, appointing Robert of Jumigas as Archbishop of Canterbury, sparking venomous anti-Norman resentment among the Saxon nobility.

19
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:32.120
[George] Edward's return to England in 1042 was greeted with widespread joy by an English populace exhausted by decades of Danish domination, who viewed the restoration of the ancient house of Surdic as a miraculous return to legitimate Saxon rule.

20
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:44.120
[George] However, forty-year-old Edward found the royal treasury exhausted and the kingdom dominated by Earl Godwin of Wessex, whose vast estates and private armies made him the true power behind the throne.

21
00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.720
[George] Edward's marriage to Godwin's daughter Edith was a tense political union.

22
00:03:48.720 --> 00:04:08.400
[George] While Edith was an accomplished, cultured queen who commissioned the Vita Edward Eregis, medieval hagiographers later claimed that Edward took a vow of lifelong chastity, refusing to consummate the marriage out of ascetic devotion, or perhaps out of sheer revulsion at the thought of producing an heir carrying Godwin's bloodline.

23
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:16.960
[George] In 1051, the smoldering feud between the king and the house of Godwin erupted into open rebellion following the Dover Fracas.

24
00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:27.920
[George] Edward's Norman brother-in-law, Count Eustace of Boulogne, visited England and was involved in a bloody street brawl with townspeople in Dover, resulting in multiple deaths.

25
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:38.640
[George] When King Edward ordered Earl Godwin to brutally punish the town of Dover without a trial, Godwin flatly refused to ravage English citizens in defence of arrogant Frenchmen.

26
00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:45.920
[George] Backed by the northern earls Leofric of Mercia and Seward of Northumbria, Edward struck with unexpected ruthlessness.

27
00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:54.000
[George] He declared Godwin and his sons outlaws, banishing them from England and sending Queen Edith to be confined in a nunnery at Werwell.

28
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.920
[George] For one brief year, Edward ruled as an absolute monarch, free of Saxon control.

29
00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:06.400
[George] The Dover Brawl of 1051 exposed the deep cultural fault line running through Edward's realm.

30
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:15.840
[George] Count Eustace of Boulogne's knights had demanded lodging in English homes at Swordpoint, sparking an armed revolt by the Dover citizenry that left twenty men dead.

31
00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:28.480
[George] When Edward ordered Earl Godwin to subject Dover to immediate military devastation without a legal trial, Godwin's principled refusal to brutalize his own English people rallied Saxon opinion to his side.

32
00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:45.960
[George] Edward, displaying an unexpected, iron-willed resolve backed by Earl Seward of Northumbria and Earl Leofric of Mercia, summoned the royal army to Gloucester, outmaneuvering Godwin and forcing the great Earl and his formidable sons into humiliating exile in Flanders and Ireland.

33
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:54.360
[George] It was during this period of solitary rule in 1051 that Edward allegedly committed the fateful act that would seal England's doom.

34
00:05:54.360 --> 00:06:14.840
[George] According to contemporary Norman chroniclers, Duke William of Normandy visited England, and Edward, childless and deeply grateful for the decades of sanctuary he had enjoyed in Normandy, secretly promised the English crown to William upon his death, dispatching Archbishop Robert of Jumagas as his personal envoy to confirm the vow.

35
00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.800
[George] However, Edward's triumph was short-lived.

36
00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:26.280
[George] In September 1052, Godwin and his formidable sons Harold and Tostig sailed up the River Thames with a massive fleet of warships.

37
00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:31.000
[George] Supported by the London citizenry, Godwin forced Edward to capitulate.

38
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:42.280
[George] The king was compelled to restore Godwin's lands, reinstate Queen Edith, and banish his beloved Norman advisers from the realm, leaving the king an isolated, titular figurehead.

39
00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:53.480
[George] The fateful promise of the English crown to Duke William of Normandy in 1051 was the pivotal diplomatic catalyst that led inexorably to the Norman conquest of 1066.

40
00:06:53.480 --> 00:07:06.920
[George] Believing he had permanently crushed the House of Godwin and knowing he had no children of his own, Edward welcomed his young, brilliant Norman cousin Duke William to England, confirming his succession promise with holy oaths.

41
00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:15.000
[George] But Edward's political naivety was brutally exposed in 1052, when Earl Godwin sailed up the Thames with a mercenary fleet.

42
00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:29.120
[George] The Saxon Thanes and citizens of London refused to fight their own countrymen to defend the king's Norman favourites, forcing Edward to banish Archbishop Robert of Jumigas and submit once more to Godwin's overbearing control.

43
00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:38.800
[George] Deprived of political supremacy, Edward poured his soul and the royal treasury into his true spiritual masterpiece, the construction of the collegiate church of St.

44
00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:40.400
[George] Peter at Westminster.

45
00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:56.480
[George] To fulfill a solemn vow he had made in exile to make a pilgrimage to Rome, a vow Pope Leo Iex commuted on condition that Edward build a great monastery, the king selected Thorny Island, a secluded marshy isle on the banks of the Thames two miles west of London.

46
00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:14.400
[George] Rejecting modest Anglo-Saxon wooden churches, Edward constructed a monumental stone cathedral in the magnificent, soaring Romanesque style, featuring massive round arches, towering stone pillars, a soaring central lantern tower, and cloistered quarters for Benedictine monks.

47
00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:25.440
[George] For thirteen years, Edward personally supervised every detail of construction, creating the architectural sanctuary where every English monarch would be crowned for the next millennium.

48
00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:38.080
[George] Following his political humiliation, King Edward channeled all his spiritual passion and financial resources into the construction of Westminster Abbey, designed as the monumental spiritual anchor of his reign.

49
00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:55.200
[George] Located on the desolate, bramble-covered Thorny Island two miles west of the noisy commercial bustle of London, Edward's Romanesque Abbey was the first great Norman-style cathedral built on English soil, constructed of pale con limestone shipped across the Channel from Normandy.

50
00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:01.800
[George] It featured massive cruciform transepts, a monumental nave, and soaring clear story windows.

51
00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:12.120
[George] The king poured a tenth of his royal income into the construction for thirteen unbroken years, building a royal palace alongside the abbey so he could attend daily monastic prayers.

52
00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:25.640
[George] When Earl Godwin died suddenly at a royal banquet in 1053, his eldest son, Harold Godwinson, succeeded as Earl of Wessex, becoming the virtual subregulus, or deputy ruler of England.

53
00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:33.000
[George] Harold was a brilliant general who campaigned across Wales and pacified the kingdom, commanding the loyalty of the Whiton.

54
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.880
[George] Meanwhile, King Edward's health failed rapidly in late 1065.

55
00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:45.000
[George] On Holy Innocence Day, December 28, 1065, Edward's life's dream was consecrated.

56
00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.880
[George] Westminster Abbey was officially dedicated with splendid liturgical ceremonies.

57
00:09:49.880 --> 00:10:00.120
[George] But the dying king was too weak to attend, confined to his bed in the adjacent palace of Westminster, tossing in feverish delirium as his court prayed for his soul.

58
00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:07.480
[George] During Edward's final decade, Earl Harold Godwinson governed England with consummate military and political brilliance.

59
00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:17.000
[George] He pacified the Welsh frontier by defeating Prince Griffith Ap Llewellyn, maintained order across the shires, and won the deep respect of the Saxon Whiton.

60
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:31.320
[George] When Harold's hot headed brother Tostig was overthrown by a popular rebellion in Northumbria in late 1065, Harold demonstrated ruthless political realism by banishing his own brother rather than plunging England into civil war.

61
00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:35.080
[George] Meanwhile, King Edward's physical strength ebbed away.

62
00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:49.520
[George] As the bishops gathered on December 28, 1065, to consecrate the magnificent Abbey Church of Westminster, Queen Edith stood at the altar representing her dying husband, who lay tossing in feverish delirium in his palace bed.

63
00:10:49.520 --> 00:11:01.440
[George] On the evening of January 5, 1066, surrounded by Queen Edith, Harold Godwinson, and Archbishop Stigend, 63-year-old King Edward woke from his coma.

64
00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:10.400
[George] Recounting a terrifying prophetic vision of two monks who warned him that England would be handed over to devils for its sins, wrapped in blood and fire.

65
00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:23.680
[George] On his deathbed, in an ambiguous gesture recorded on the Bayou Tapestry, Edward extended his frail hand toward Harold, whispering, To thee, Harold my brother, I commit my kingdom and my wife.

66
00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:28.800
[George] Passing away that night, Edward was buried the following morning in Westminster Abbey.

67
00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:40.640
[George] Hours later, Harold Godwinson was crowned King of England, while across the English Channel, Duke William of Normandy erupted in fury, claiming Edward had promised the crown to him.

68
00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:52.800
[George] Canonized as Saint Edward the Confessor in 1161, his death marked the tragic end of five centuries of Anglo-Saxon rule, plunging England into the fateful bloodbath of 1066.

69
00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:58.560
[George] The ambiguous deathbed scene of King Edward on January 5, 1066.

70
00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:06.320
[George] Remains the supreme enigma of English history, as the dying king extended his trembling hand toward Harold Godwinson.

71
00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:16.160
[George] He uttered words that could be interpreted as either a full royal bequest of the kingdom or merely a temporary trusteeship until Duke William could claim his inheritance.

72
00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:31.800
[George] Buried before the high altar of his newly consecrated abbey on January 6, Edward's passing was marked by the coronation of Harold Godwinson as King Harold I.I., sparking the furious wrath of Duke William of Normandy and King Harald Hardrada of Norway.

73
00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:47.080
[George] Canonized by Pope Alexander III in 1161, Saint Edward the Confessor remains the patron saint of the royal family, a gentle, pious king whose holy abbey became the stage for a thousand years of English royal history.

74
00:12:50.920 --> 00:13:00.680
[Rebecca] Edward the Confessor's Piety, the founding of Westminster Abbey, and the disputed succession unleashed the Norman conquest that transformed British civilization.

75
00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:08.120
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the fiery conqueror himself, William the Conqueror and the Battle of Hastings.

76
00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:11.400
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

